 seeking another term", "All ten expert ratings place the race at Safe/Solid or Likely R", "Overwhelming fundraising gap: Franklin has $960K on hand to Gibson's $1.5K", "No public polling; Polymarket prices Franklin at 92%"], "rationale": "Franklin holds a strongly Republican seat, a massive cash advantage, and a unanimous expert consensus ranging from Likely R to Safe R, with markets pricing his win at 92%. With no polling and only token Democratic fundraising, the race sits firmly in Safe R territory.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic outside spending or a major scandal involving Franklin would be the only realistic path to narrowing this race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Scott Franklin (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Incumbent Republican Scott Franklin is heavily favored in Florida's 18th District, a seat with an eight-point Republican lean that he has already won twice. His Democratic challenger, Curtis Gibson, has raised about $12,000 and reported under $1,500 on hand, while Franklin has roughly $960,000 in cash. An independent candidate is also on the ballot but is similarly underfunded.\n\nEvery major race rater calls the district Safe or Solid Republican, with only a couple of Likely R ratings, and prediction markets give Franklin about a 92% chance of winning. No public general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings, and money, all of which point the same direction. Barring an unexpected development, Franklin is positioned for an easy reelection.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0485, "margin": -10.89}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0328, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0147, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0331, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0338, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0452, "margin": -12.0}, {"date